# APK Milestone Semantic Audit (optional, manual only)

This audit asks whether a set of completed APK tasks agrees with the current canonical
architecture, domain, and interface contracts, and whether those task contracts leave semantic
gaps between them. Task-local verification, review, and gate can each be valid while integration
exposes an assumption no single task owned.

It is a cross-contract analysis of already-completed work. It is not a generic code reviewer, a
task-local recertification, or a new completion certificate.

## When to use it

- Only on an explicit human request, for example "Run APK milestone semantic audit for tasks
  0033-0042" or "Audit the completed milestone since <ref> against the current canonical
  architecture/domain contracts".
- Never activate automatically. No after-task trigger, scanner, work/review hook, mandatory
  release or completion gate, or heuristic may invoke it, and no task may require it.
- Ordinary work continues to complete through the normal APK work, verification, review, gate,
  and done flow without any audit state or evidence.

## Resolve a visible, finite selection

Before inspecting anything, turn the request into a visible finite set of completed tasks:

- an explicit task-ID list or range;
- a Git-ref-bounded milestone, resolved to its completed task membership;
- for a mid-milestone request, only the completed portion, with relevant unfinished
  dependencies/consumers read as declared boundary context only.

Missing, ambiguous, or over-broad refs/membership/dependencies require clarification or an
explicitly limited result. Never silently widen into an unbounded repository scan, never certify
unfinished implementation, and never present Git activity alone as task membership.

## Bounded inspection

Use existing APK primitives rather than inventing new ones:

- `apk task deps <task-id>` for dependencies and dependents;
- `apk context <task-id> --budget <units>` for a deterministic required/relevant/optional pack;
- `apk suggest-context "<description>"` for optional candidates only;
- canonical docs, task contracts, bounded provenance/evidence/gate summaries, relevant
  interfaces, and integration tests.

Context output is a selection interface; read the selected files before reasoning. Required
context is never silently evicted on overflow. Read a specifically relevant completed/archived
contract by its established reference; do not globally enable history or dump ignored runtime
stores. Distinguish immutable completion-time evidence from current freshness, and
task-attributed changes from broad Git activity. Unavailable history stays explicitly unknown.

## Cross-contract reasoning targets

Look for integration-level contradictions and missing invariants, for example:

- contradictory assumptions between two locally valid tasks;
- multiple sources of truth or ownership/reference identity mismatch;
- occurrence-versus-recording time and persistence ordering disagreements;
- transaction boundaries and lifecycle correction ownership;
- migration compatibility and exhaustive schema evolution;
- shared units, value representations, and overflow behavior;
- error semantics, concurrency, and security boundaries;
- one task relying on a guarantee its peer never supplies.

## Bounded findings

Return findings that are traceable and falsifiable. Each finding should name:

- the involved task IDs;
- canonical contract and code/test references;
- the contradictory assumption or missing invariant;
- an observable counterexample and its impact;
- confidence and any unresolved evidence;
- a proposed narrowly scoped corrective/follow-up contract with acceptance boundaries,
  dependencies, and overlap checks.

Separate supported defects from hypotheses, documentation drift, and accepted tradeoffs. State
coverage and omissions explicitly. A clean result never claims exhaustive semantic correctness,
and deterministic instruction tests never prove arbitrary model reasoning.

## Proposal, approval, and historical integrity

Present the bounded findings and the concrete proposed backlog, with intended paths, scope,
acceptance boundaries, dependencies, and overlap checks, before any write. A skill invocation or
an individual answer is not approval to write. Require explicit human approval of the concrete
proposal; silence or refusal means no writes, and partial approval authorizes only the accepted
new contracts and necessary canonical planning references.

New work receives fresh task IDs through canonical task creation and graph validation. Never
rewrite or mutate historical done contracts or evidence, never automatically reopen completed
work, and never fix product/runtime code during an audit.

## Hard boundaries

- No automatic, continuous, or heuristic-triggered audit.
- No full-repository LLM scan.
- No mandatory release/completion gate, extra mandatory review pass, new worker role, task
  lifecycle state, persistent database, ontology, issue tracker, evidence schema, provider/model
  runtime, or daemon.
- No new CLI merely to name the feature.
- No historical contract/evidence mutation, automatic reopen, or product implementation.
- No claim of arbitrary-LLM reasoning-quality guarantees or exhaustive system correctness.

## Relation to task grill and task review

- Task grill clarifies one task before implementation.
- Milestone semantic audit analyses semantic consistency across multiple completed contracts
  after or during a milestone.
- Per-task review remains authoritative for its own contract.

They are distinct, optional, and independently invoked; neither mandates the other.

## Invocation note for harnesses

This asset is plain Markdown shipped inside the installed package under
`dist/core/templates/skills/apk-milestone-semantic-audit/SKILL.md.hbs`. A harness without native
skill discovery must read that installed asset explicitly when a human invokes milestone audit.
It works with local models and compatible harnesses; APK adds no installer, model routing,
provider integration, or user-global skill directory write.
